<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="efe3ea65">&lt;p&gt;
    A statement of what business results must be delivered to provide value by meeting business needs and objectives
    (Goldsmith). Business requirements are changes needed by the business but may not be part of a system to be
    implemented. They can include measures and motivation requirements, organizational requirements, and physical
    plant requirements. They can also identify and define changes outside of a system, such as process or organizational
    structure change, that the business must address to support the overall business solution.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Examples:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        A customer must be able to purchase from either our on-line or retail outlet, and pickup or return an item through
        either channel
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Our 3PL providers need to be aligned with the new future state operating model
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Provide a 360-degree information view of the customer experience
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="5921a70"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="83cf1689">&lt;p&gt;
    As business requirements are analyzed, and refined, additional details may emerge that will have an influence on
    &lt;em&gt;other&lt;/em&gt; business requirements and system requirements. Additionally, if the requirement is part of a new
    capability for an organization, it may be further categorized into an Operating Model dimension.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Some business requirements may be transitional, meaning they are one-time or short-lived requirements needed to
    transition to the future state. Some examples include new branding, product merchandising display requirements,
    new/modified facilities, sites, physical plant and equipment, labor agreement changes, policy changes, or permits and
    license agreements. In practice, they could be flagged as transitional in a requirements register.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="95363b2a"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="d97d4d44">&lt;p&gt;
    To formally describe what business results must be delivered to provide value by meeting business objectives (problem,
    opportunity, or challenge), &lt;em&gt;outside&lt;/em&gt; of the technology enablement of a solution.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
